Notebook[{

Cell[CellGroupData[{
Cell[TextData[StyleBox["Young Tableaux",
 FontColor->RGBColor[1, 0, 0]]], "Title"],

Cell["Jos\[EAcute] M. Mart\[IAcute]n-Garc\[IAcute]a", "Subsubtitle"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[TextData[{
 "0. ",
 StyleBox["Comments",
  FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1]]
}], "Subsection",
 FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
0.1. Applications of Young tableaux\
\>", "Subsubsection"],

Cell["\<\
Combinatorics. It is possible to convert the set of all tableaux into a \
monoid in several forms:
\t
\t- The Schensted \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]bumping\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] \
algorithm and the Sch\[UDoubleDot]tzenberger \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]sliding\
\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] algorithm.
\t- The relations with words developed by Knuth and Lascoux, and Sch\
\[UDoubleDot]tzenberger.
\t- The Robinson-Schensted-Knuth correspondence between matrices with \
nonnegative integer entries and pairs of tableaux on the same shape.

These constructions are used for the combinatorial version of the \
Littlewood-Richardson rule, and for counting the numbers of tableaux of \
various types.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell["\<\
The algebra of symmetric functions.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell["\<\
Representations of the symmetric and general linear groups.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell["\<\
Tensor decompositions and symbolic tensor canonicalization.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell["Geometry of flag varieties.", "Text"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

