Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["3. Young tableaux", "Subsection",
 FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
3.0. Comments on the notions of tableau\
\>", "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "We can visualize a partition as a 2D arrangement of ",
 StyleBox["boxes",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 ". Given a set of ",
 StyleBox["labels",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " (Fulton calls this set an ",
 StyleBox["alphabet",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " [m]={1,...,m} because he always has in mind these numeric labels)",
 StyleBox[",",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " a Young tableau is a mapping from that set of boxes to that set of labels. \
In general the mapping is neither injective nor surjective. The sets of boxes \
and labels may have different cardinalities (including 0), though the number \
of boxes must be always finite. Fulton talks about a ",
 StyleBox["numbering",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " of a tableau when the labels are all different, and a ",
 StyleBox["filling",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " when they can be repeated.\n\nThere are different types of tableaux, and \
there is no agreement in the literature on how to name them. For example \
(copied from Fulton, page 2):\n\nYoung diagrams are also known as Ferrers \
diagrams or frames; sometimes dots are used instead of boxes, and sometimes, \
particularly in France, they are written upside down, in order not to offend \
Descartes. What we call tableaux are known variously as semistandard \
tableaux, or column-strict tableaux, or generalized Young tableaux (in which \
case our standard tableaux are just called tableaux). Combinatorialists also \
know them as column-strict reversed plane partitions; the \"reversed\" is in \
opposition to the case of decreasing rows and columns, which was studied \
first (and allows zero entries).\n\nThis is our current nomenclature:\n\n- \
Any filling of a Young diagram (aka Young frame) is a tableau, with head \
Tableau.\n- If the labels of a tableau are all different (checked with \
UnsameQ) then we call it a \"disjoint tableau\" (I have invented that name).\n\
- If the columns are strictly sorted and the rows are sorted (but could \
contain repeated labels) then we call it a \"semistandard tableau\".\n- If \
the tableau is disjoint and both the columns and rows are strictly sorted \
then we call it a \"standard tableau\".\n- If the flattening of all rows \
gives a strictly sorted list of labels then we call it a \"normal tableau\". \
This could also be called a \"first tableau\" (in the lexicographic ordering \
of labels provided by Sort)."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "It would be nice to have a RandomXTableau function (X meaning any of the \
previous types). ",
 StyleBox["Combinatorica",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " has one for standard tableaux, but it is rather complicated."
}], "Text"],

Cell["\<\
We now remove empty lists from Tableau:\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"Tableau", "[", 
    RowBox[{"L___", ",", 
     RowBox[{"{", "}"}], ",", "R___"}], "]"}], ":=", 
   RowBox[{"Tableau", "[", 
    RowBox[{"L", ",", "R"}], "]"}]}], ";"}]], "Input",
 InitializationCell->True],

Cell["\<\
Any tableau defines a partition:\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"TableauPartition", "[", "tab_Tableau", "]"}], ":=", 
   RowBox[{"Length", "/@", 
    RowBox[{"List", "@@", "tab"}]}]}], ";"}]], "Input"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["3.1. Ordering of tableaux", "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The set of labels is always assumed to have a total order. There are two \
ways to interpret this. We could take the ordering of the input set, or we \
can declare that the ordering is always decided by ",
 StyleBox["Mathematica'",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " s Sort function (that is, lexicographic order). Currently we take the \
latter approach, because it is simpler and faster.\n\nThe ordering of the \
labels of the set induces an ordering of the tableaux for a given partition \
shape. It compares succesive elements of the first row and then succesive \
rows, until one label differs. The comparison decides the ordering. If we use \
Sort for the labels then fortunately Sort also gives the right order for \
tableaux. The listing functions XTableaux will follow this ordering. In case \
we want to change the sorting function of labels, we shall use this driver:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"LabelsSort", "=", "Sort"}], ";"}]], "Input",
 InitializationCell->True],

Cell["\<\
That is, all functions using a list of labels will call first \
LabelsSort[labels] to bring the set to a canonical form. There is an \
important question here: what happens if we provide a set of labels with \
repeated elements? I have the impression that the function LabelsSort should \
be Union, and not just Sort. In other words, do we work with a set of labels \
or with a multiset of labels? QUESTION.\
\>", "Text"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
3.2. Tableau and its formatting\
\>", "Subsubsection"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"?", "Tableau"}]], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
 StyleBox["\<\"Tableau is the head used to denote a Young tableau. Its \
arguments are lists containing the labels of consecutive rows of the tableau. \
A tableau is typeset as a left-justified and top-justified ragged array of \
boxes containing the labels (English style).\"\>", 
  "MSG"]], "Print", "PrintUsage",
 CellTags->"Info3579442054-9818602"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["\<\
This is a very simply way of formatting, and we should look for something \
better (now not evaluatable).\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"Format", "[", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"tab", ":", 
      RowBox[{"Tableau", "[", "__List", "]"}]}], ",", "StandardForm"}], "]"}],
    ":=", 
   RowBox[{"DisplayForm", "@", 
    RowBox[{"GridBox", "[", 
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{"Module", "[", 
       RowBox[{
        RowBox[{"{", "pad", "}"}], ",", 
        RowBox[{
         RowBox[{"PadRight", "[", 
          RowBox[{
           RowBox[{"List", "@@", "tab"}], ",", "pad"}], "]"}], "/.", " ", 
         RowBox[{"pad", "->", "\"\<\>\""}]}]}], "]"}], ",", 
      RowBox[{"ColumnSpacings", "\[Rule]", "0.3"}], ",", 
      RowBox[{"RowSpacings", "\[Rule]", "0.3"}]}], "]"}]}]}], ";"}]], "Input",
 
 Evaluatable->False],

Cell["\<\
New code (no use yet of InterpretationBox or TemplateBoxes) :\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"Format", "[", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"Tableau", "[", "rows__List", "]"}], ",", "StandardForm"}], "]"}],
   ":=", 
  RowBox[{"Text", "@", 
   RowBox[{"Style", "[", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"Grid", "[", 
      RowBox[{"Map", "[", 
       RowBox[{
        RowBox[{
         RowBox[{"Item", "[", 
          RowBox[{"#", ",", 
           RowBox[{"Frame", "\[Rule]", "Blue"}]}], "]"}], "&"}], ",", 
        RowBox[{"{", "rows", "}"}], ",", 
        RowBox[{"{", "2", "}"}]}], "]"}], "]"}], ",", 
     RowBox[{"FontSize", "\[Rule]", "10"}]}], "]"}]}]}]], "Input",
 InitializationCell->True],

Cell["\<\
Boxes do not need to have the same width or height :\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"Tableau", "[", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{"a", ",", "100000", ",", "2", ",", "d"}], "}"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"e", "^", 
      RowBox[{"e", "^", 
       RowBox[{"e", "^", "e"}]}]}], ",", "f"}], "}"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"{", "X", "}"}]}], "]"}]], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
 InterpretationBox[Cell[BoxData[
   FormBox[
    StyleBox[
     TagBox[GridBox[{
        {
         ItemBox["a",
          Frame->RGBColor[0, 0, 1],
          StripOnInput->False], 
         ItemBox["100000",
          Frame->RGBColor[0, 0, 1],
          StripOnInput->False], 
         ItemBox["2",
          Frame->RGBColor[0, 0, 1],
          StripOnInput->False], 
         ItemBox["d",
          Frame->RGBColor[0, 0, 1],
          StripOnInput->False]},
        {
         ItemBox[
          SuperscriptBox["e", 
           SuperscriptBox["e", 
            SuperscriptBox["e", "e"]]],
          Frame->RGBColor[0, 0, 1],
          StripOnInput->False], 
         ItemBox["f",
          Frame->RGBColor[0, 0, 1],
          StripOnInput->False], "", ""},
        {
         ItemBox["X",
          Frame->RGBColor[0, 0, 1],
          StripOnInput->False], "", "", ""}
       },
       AutoDelete->False,
       GridBoxItemSize->{"Columns" -> {{Automatic}}, "Rows" -> {{Automatic}}}],
      "Grid"],
     StripOnInput->False,
     FontSize->10], TextForm]], "InlineText"],
  Text[
   Style[
    Grid[{{
       Item[Tableaux`Private`a, Frame -> RGBColor[0, 0, 1]], 
       Item[100000, Frame -> RGBColor[0, 0, 1]], 
       Item[2, Frame -> RGBColor[0, 0, 1]], 
       Item[Tableaux`Private`d, Frame -> RGBColor[0, 0, 1]]}, {
       Item[
       Tableaux`Private`e^(
         Tableaux`Private`e^(Tableaux`Private`e^Tableaux`Private`e)), Frame -> 
        RGBColor[0, 0, 1]], 
       Item[Tableaux`Private`f, Frame -> RGBColor[0, 0, 1]]}, {
       Item[Tableaux`Private`X, Frame -> RGBColor[0, 0, 1]]}}], FontSize -> 
    10]]]], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["\<\
Empty lists would leave a gap, but now we remove them :\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"Tableau", "[", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{"a", ",", "b", ",", "c"}], "}"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"{", "}"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"{", "d", "}"}]}], "]"}]], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
 InterpretationBox[Cell[BoxData[
   FormBox[
    StyleBox[
     TagBox[GridBox[{
        {
         ItemBox["a",
          Frame->RGBColor[0, 0, 1],
          StripOnInput->False], 
         ItemBox["b",
          Frame->RGBColor[0, 0, 1],
          StripOnInput->False], 
         ItemBox["c",
          Frame->RGBColor[0, 0, 1],
          StripOnInput->False]},
        {
         ItemBox["d",
          Frame->RGBColor[0, 0, 1],
          StripOnInput->False], "", ""}
       },
       AutoDelete->False,
       GridBoxItemSize->{"Columns" -> {{Automatic}}, "Rows" -> {{Automatic}}}],
      "Grid"],
     StripOnInput->False,
     FontSize->10], TextForm]], "InlineText"],
  Text[
   Style[
    Grid[{{
       Item[Tableaux`Private`a, Frame -> RGBColor[0, 0, 1]], 
       Item[Tableaux`Private`b, Frame -> RGBColor[0, 0, 1]], 
       Item[Tableaux`Private`c, Frame -> RGBColor[0, 0, 1]]}, {
       Item[Tableaux`Private`d, Frame -> RGBColor[0, 0, 1]]}}], FontSize -> 
    10]]]], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["\<\
This is not formatted, because its only reasonable formatting would be a \
blank, and that is a really bad idea. It would be very difficult to \
distinguish { } from { Tableau[ ] }.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"Tableau", "[", "]"}]], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"Tableau", "[", "]"}]], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["\<\
It is simple to generalize the formatting to \"unfilled tableaux\", or \
\"Young diagrams\" :\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"Format", "[", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"Tableau", "[", "lengths__Integer", "]"}], ",", "StandardForm"}], 
   "]"}], ":=", 
  RowBox[{"Text", "@", 
   RowBox[{"Style", "[", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"Grid", "[", 
      RowBox[{"Map", "[", 
       RowBox[{
        RowBox[{
         RowBox[{"Item", "[", 
          RowBox[{"#", ",", 
           RowBox[{"Frame", "\[Rule]", "Blue"}]}], "]"}], "&"}], ",", 
        RowBox[{
         RowBox[{
          RowBox[{"Table", "[", 
           RowBox[{"\"\<  \>\"", ",", 
