Cell["\<\
3) There is no obvious hook-length formula. Let us not define a counting \
function here...\
\>", "Text"]
}, Closed]]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["5. Tableau computations", "Subsection",
 FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["5.0. Comments", "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "This section follows [",
 ButtonBox["Fulton",
  BaseStyle->"Hyperlink",
  ButtonData->{
    URL["http://www.amazon.com/Young-Tableaux-Applications-Representation-\
Mathematical/dp/0521567246/"], None},
  ButtonNote->
   "http://www.amazon.com/Young-Tableaux-Applications-Representation-\
Mathematical/dp/0521567246/"],
 "], chapters 1-5."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The discussion goes as follows:\n\t1) There is a non-commutative \
associative product of semistandard tableaux, giving another semistandard \
tableau with the joining (in a nontrivial way) of the boxes. It is called the \
Schensted product, and makes the set of semistandard tableaux into an algebra \
over \[DoubleStruckCapitalZ]. The idea can be implemented in alternative \
ways, using rectification of skew-tableaux, the RSK correspondence with the \
plactic monoid of words, or the Schur polynomials, among others.\n\t2) The \
condition of being semistandard is so restrictive that usually we do not need \
to know where the labels are, but only how many of each there are: this is \
the weight \[Mu]={",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["\[Mu]", "1"], TraditionalForm]]],
 ", ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["\[Mu]", "2"], TraditionalForm]]],
 ",...} of a semistandard tableau: there are ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["\[Mu]", "1"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " 1\[CloseCurlyQuote]s, ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["\[Mu]", "2"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " 2\[CloseCurlyQuote]s, etc.\n\t3) Kostka numbers ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["K", "\[Lambda]\[Mu]"], TraditionalForm]]],
 ": number of semistandard tableaux with shape \[Lambda] and content \[Mu] \
(treat as another partition, because ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["K", "\[Lambda]\[Mu]"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " is invariant under reorderings of \[Mu]). Sorting partitions \
lexicographically K is lower triangular and with 1\[CloseCurlyQuote]s in the \
diagonal.\n\t4) The main question is: given all semistandard tableaux of two \
given shapes, which other shapes can we obtain in their Schensted products, \
and with which multiplicities? Note that this is a property of the two \
initial shapes (partitions) only. The LR number ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox[
    SuperscriptBox[
     SubscriptBox["c", "\[Lambda]"], "\[Nu]"], "\[Mu]"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " is the number of ways of writing a given semistandard tableau of shape \
\[Nu] as the Schensted product of semistandard tableaux of respective shapes \
\[Lambda] and \[Mu]. It is also the number of semistandard skew-tableaux \
\[Nu]/\[Lambda] with weight \[Mu].\n\t5) Given partitions \[Lambda] and \
\[Mu], the LR rule gives the ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox[
    SuperscriptBox[
     SubscriptBox["c", "\[Lambda]"], "\[Nu]"], "\[Mu]"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " for all \[Nu], using a backtracking algorithm. It is also possible to \
write another backtracking algorithm that computes them for all \[Mu], given \
\[Lambda] and \[Nu]."
}], "Text"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
5.1. Bumping and the Schensted product\
\>", "Subsubsection"],

Cell["\<\
1) This operation (called \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]row insertion\
\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] or \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]row bumping\
\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote]) is well defined only on semistandard tableaux, and \
the result is always still semistandard.

Take a semistandard tableau T and a label x. Construct a new semistandard \
tableau, denoted T\[LeftArrow]x, with one more box than T, with some moving \
around:
\t1) If x is at least as large as all the entries in the first row of T, \
simply add x in a new box to the end of the first row.
\t2) If not, find the left-most entry in the first row that is strictly \
larger than x. Put x in the box of this entry and remov (\
\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]bump\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote]) the entry.
\t3) Take this entry that was bumped from the first row and repeat the \
process on the second row.
\t4) Keep going until the bumped entry can be put at the end of the row it is \
bumped into, or until it is bumped out the bottom, in which case it forms a \
new row with one entry.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"?", "RowBump"}]], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
 StyleBox["\<\"RowBump[tab, x, n] bumps the label x into the n-th row of the \
semistandard tableau tab. RowBump[tab, x] bumps the label into the first row \
of tab.\"\>", "MSG"]], "Print", "PrintUsage",
 CellTags->"Info3579442348-9818602"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[BoxData[{
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"ColumnOfStrictlyLarger", "[", 
    RowBox[{"rowlabels_", ",", "label_"}], "]"}], ":=", 
   RowBox[{"Module", "[", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"{", 
      RowBox[{"col", "=", 
       RowBox[{"Length", "[", "rowlabels", "]"}]}], "}"}], ",", 
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{"While", "[", 
       RowBox[{
        RowBox[{
         RowBox[{"!", 
          RowBox[{"OrderedQ", "[", 
           RowBox[{"{", 
            RowBox[{
             RowBox[{"rowlabels", "[", 
              RowBox[{"[", "col", "]"}], "]"}], ",", "label"}], "}"}], 
           "]"}]}], "&&", 
         RowBox[{"col", ">", "0"}]}], ",", 
        RowBox[{"col", "--"}]}], "]"}], ";", 
      RowBox[{"col", "+", "1"}]}]}], "]"}]}], ";"}], "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"RowBump", "[", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"tab_Tableau", "?", "SemistandardTableauQ"}], ",", "label_", ",", 
     RowBox[{"row_:", "1"}]}], "]"}], ":=", 
   RowBox[{"With", "[", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"{", 
      RowBox[{"col", "=", 
       RowBox[{"If", "[", 
        RowBox[{
         RowBox[{"row", "\[LessEqual]", 
          RowBox[{"Length", "[", "tab", "]"}]}], ",", 
         RowBox[{"ColumnOfStrictlyLarger", "[", 
          RowBox[{
           RowBox[{"tab", "[", 
            RowBox[{"[", "row", "]"}], "]"}], ",", "label"}], "]"}], ",", 
         "0"}], "]"}]}], "}"}], ",", 
     RowBox[{"Which", "[", "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"col", "===", "0"}], ",", "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
       RowBox[{"Append", "[", 
        RowBox[{"tab", ",", 
         RowBox[{"{", "label", "}"}]}], "]"}], ",", "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
       RowBox[{"col", ">", 
        RowBox[{"Length", "[", 
         RowBox[{"tab", "[", 
          RowBox[{"[", "row", "]"}], "]"}], "]"}]}], ",", 
       RowBox[{"MapAt", "[", 
        RowBox[{
         RowBox[{
          RowBox[{"Append", "[", 
           RowBox[{"#", ",", "label"}], "]"}], "&"}], ",", "tab", ",", 
         "row"}], "]"}], ",", "\[IndentingNewLine]", "True", ",", 
       "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
       RowBox[{"RowBump", "[", 
        RowBox[{
         RowBox[{"ReplacePart", "[", 
          RowBox[{"tab", ",", 
           RowBox[{
            RowBox[{"{", 
             RowBox[{"row", ",", "col"}], "}"}], "\[Rule]", "label"}]}], 
          "]"}], ",", 
         RowBox[{"tab", "[", 
          RowBox[{"[", 
           RowBox[{"row", ",", "col"}], "]"}], "]"}], ",", 
         RowBox[{"row", "+", "1"}]}], "]"}]}], "\[IndentingNewLine]", "]"}]}],
     "\[IndentingNewLine]", "]"}]}], ";"}]}], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
Example from Fulton: The added 2 bumps 3 from the first row, which bumps a 5 \
from the second row, which bumps 6 from the third row, which gets placed in \
the final row.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"Tableau", "[", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{"1", ",", "2", ",", "2", ",", "3"}], "}"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{"2", ",", "3", ",", "5", ",", "5"}], "}"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{"4", ",", "4", ",", "6"}], "}"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{"5", ",", "6"}], "}"}]}], "]"}]], "Input"],

